# debian/patches/series -- EMPTY, and this file exists rather than being absent.
#
# 3.0 (quilt) applies whatever debian/patches/series lists, so this is where a
# packaging-local patch would be registered. It is empty because irc-serve builds
# from its own unmodified source: there is no patch to carry.
#
# EVERY LINE HERE IS A COMMENT, and that is valid rather than a near miss:
# Dpkg::Source::Quilt::read_patch_list() strips `#` comments and skips the lines
# that are left empty, so an all-comment file parses as an empty series. A reader
# who wants to check that claim has the function name in this sentence for exactly
# that reason.
#
# THE FILE IS ALSO NOT REQUIRED, which is a correction to what an earlier draft of
# this comment asserted. That draft claimed a missing series file makes dpkg-source
# fail with "cannot apply patches". It does not: read_patch_list() returns an empty
# list when the file does not exist, so both an absent file and this one mean "no
# patches". The file is kept anyway because a maintainer looking for where to put a
# patch should find the answer rather than have to know the format's internals.
#
# A previous draft of this comment was wrong about dpkg-source in the other
# direction too: it credited the series file with making 3.0 (quilt) work, when it
# is debian/source/format that selects the format. A comment that states a fact
# about a tool backwards is worse than no comment, so both are recorded here.